---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="About Cove"
  description="Why Cove exists, what kind of local media organizer it is, and who it is built for."
  imagePath="/images/screenshots/dashboard-home.png"
  schemaPageType="AboutPage"
>
  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow-small">About Cove</p>
        <h1 class="page-heading">A serious personal library, without the cloud</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Cove is for people who keep their media on their own hardware and want it to be as nice to use as any streaming app.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Why it exists</h2>
        <p>
          Most media software picks a side. It's either simple but shallow, or powerful but miserable for the first week. Cove tries to be easy on day one and still have the depth waiting when a library gets big, detailed, or shared.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner info-grid">
      <article class="info-card">
        <h2>Local-first</h2>
        <p>Your media, metadata, previews, and accounts stay on the machine you control.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="info-card">
        <h2>Built around everyday use</h2>
        <p>Searching, browsing, tagging, and picking up where you left off are the core of the app.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="info-card">
        <h2>Extensible by design</h2>
        <p>Downloaders, scrapers, jobs, themes, extensions are built-in from the ground up, not an afterthought.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner">
      <p class="section-kicker">A low floor and a high ceiling</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">Useful in the first hour, still growing years later.</h2>
      <p class="page-copy">
        You can install Cove, point it at a folder, and use the library before learning a single advanced concept. Search, tags, filters, and watch history are just there. The rest stays out of your way until you want it: tagging inside videos, more media types, accounts and permissions for sharing, downloader tools, and an extension system for whatever's missing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
